    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` address family under the interface's unit. The equivalent of Cisco's `ipv6 enable` is to simply declare the IPv6 address family (`family inet6`) on the interface. I've included both interface configurations since both had `ipv6 enable` in the original Cisco config.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` address family under the interface's unit. The equivalent of Cisco's `ipv6 enable` is to simply declare the IPv6 address family (`family inet6`) on the interface. I've included both interface configurations since both had `ipv6 enable` in the original Cisco config.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::1/64\\n##', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::1/64'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 ospf [parameter1] area [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ospf3 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols ospf3 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, OSPFv3 is configured under `protocols ospf3` (not `ipv6 ospf` like Cisco). The interface is specified separately under the area configuration. The equivalent command enables OSPFv3 on the interface and assigns it to area 0.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ospf3 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 pim [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols pim interface <interface-name> mode sparse', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols pim interface <interface-name> mode sparse\\n##\\n\\nNote: In the Juniper command, you would replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., \"ge-1/0/0.0\" or \"ge-1/0/4.0\") where you want to enable IPv6 PIM sparse mode. Juniper handles IPv4 and IPv6 PIM configurations under the same PIM protocol hierarchy.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols pim interface <interface-name> mode sparse'}]]": 1,
