    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Cisco \"ipv6 enable\" command enables IPv6 processing on an interface\\n- In Juniper, this is equivalent to configuring the \"family inet6\" (IPv6) under the interface\\'s logical unit\\n- Since the original configuration shows two interfaces (GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet1/0/4) with IPv6 enabled, both need the equivalent Juniper configuration\\n- Juniper interface naming uses \"ge\" instead of \"GigabitEthernet\"', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Cisco \"ipv6 enable\" command enables IPv6 processing on an interface\\n- In Juniper, this is equivalent to configuring the \"family inet6\" (IPv6) under the interface\\'s logical unit\\n- Since the original configuration shows two interfaces (GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet1/0/4) with IPv6 enabled, both need the equivalent Juniper configuration\\n- Juniper interface naming uses \"ge\" instead of \"GigabitEthernet\"', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
